{
  "A": "Research exact integer trial division. Replace floating-point sqrtl/fmod and testing every integer with integer remainders and safe stopping conditions. Compare a low-setup odd/6k±1 loop against division by a sieve-generated prime list through sqrt(maximum candidate), at most 24514 under the contract. Use appropriately sized unsigned arithmetic with proven bounds. Measure initialization overhead, especially for small P; do not assume the prime table always wins. Produce a standalone correct variant and report unchanged-harness timings.",
  "B": "Research deterministic Miller–Rabin as an independent primality kernel. Bases 2, 7 and 61 suffice throughout the documented numeric range. Handle small values, small-prime equality and bases divisible by n correctly. uint64_t multiplication safely contains products of residues below 600971707, so modular exponentiation needs neither floating point nor arbitrary precision. Compare a modest small-prime prefilter against no prefilter, accounting for its setup and repeated work. Retain exact deterministic correctness, not probabilistic acceptance, and measure the complete program.",
  "C": "Research candidate-generation and filtering overhead. Parse fragments once in decimal and construct n = left * 10000000 + right, preserving the seven-character suffix width despite leading zeros. Compare straightforward per-candidate small-prime filters with precomputed fragment residues and row bitmasks: P <= 30 permits one uint32_t candidate mask per row. Every surviving candidate must still receive an exact primality test; divisibility filters must preserve prime-equality cases. Separately evaluate grouped duplicate fragments with multiplicities and sorted numeric enumeration, which can avoid repeated tests and final result sorting. Buffered I/O is a measured secondary experiment, not an assumed improvement. Never discard pairs through greedy matching or deduplicate output occurrences.",
  "G": "Integrate only correctness-passing programs produced by A/B/C. Retain the fastest standalone variant as a control, then measure selective combinations such as A+C and B+C; remove duplicated filters and include all setup, parsing and output costs. Integration can regress, so require fresh full-program measurements rather than adding predicted gains. Pay particular attention to small-P overhead; any size-based dispatch must use legitimate runtime properties, not case identities. Use the unchanged research harness for selection and distinct confirmation cases for validation, without tuning to confirmation values. Preserve sorted decimal output, multiplicities and exact newline formatting. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target or permissions.",
  "analysis": "The only measured result is a correct baseline averaging 0.04431629957010349 seconds, with score 0.5; there is no comparative or profiling evidence yet. Source inspection suggests the floating-point remainder loop is the principal optimization opportunity, while repeated string concatenation/conversion is avoidable secondary work. The certified contract permits at most 900 pair candidates, fixed-width numeric concatenation and values below 2^30. Correctness means emitting the sorted multiset of every prime concatenation, not solving a matching problem. Use only these documented structural guarantees, never private values or expected answers. Focus the three research trees on integer trial division, deterministic Miller–Rabin, and amortized candidate processing respectively. Claim correctness for the certified contract only, not the contradictory example or unrestricted contest domain."
